><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250701093837+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="b82fff9b-d5b1-4a41-afb5-b0fbe0558c25" date="D:20250701093837+10'00'" name="c60e9007-a0ee-4b97-a585-d0d67cefc6f1" icon="Comment" page="7" rect="58.773865,539.176758,82.773865,563.176758" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Coaches and practitioners should examine athlete perceptions&#xD;of the effectiveness and importance of recovery&#xD;strategies. An individualised plan to optimise recovery in&#xD;training and competition can then be developed.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-11.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>● </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Swimmer’s and coaches’ perceived importance of recovery&#xD;varied between training and competition, potentially&#xD;indicating a lack of understanding of how to correctly&#xD;programme recovery. Therefore, education should focus&#xD;on recovery planning and periodization. (Mujika et al.,&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2018</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>)&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-11.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>● </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Targeted education for swimmers and coaches is required&#xD;and should focus on available and applicable recovery strategies,&#xD;and deliver this content through a mixture of workshops,&#xD;social networks, and personal interactions (Fullagar&#xD;et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2019</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). Education of swimmers and coaches should be&#xD;conducted both separately, to ensure content is targeted to&#xD;and progresses the appropriate knowledge level, and combined,&#xD;to promote recovery strategy implementation.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-11.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>● </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Sport scientists can work towards the removal of potential&#xD;barriers for athletes and coaches by providing: targeted&#xD;education to advance strategy knowledge, access to&#xD;equipment or resources not otherwise available, and&#xD;assist with integration and practical application of strategies.&#xD;Sport scientists enable the development of individualised&#xD;training and competition recovery plans that are&#xD;informed by current evidence-based practice guidelines.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
